# Source-analysis focus for this run (read before writing your digest): ${vault_tag}

The deliverable is a FAO water tenure catalogue for ${area}, categorised by tenure type, with a place
focus. In your digest, beyond the standard structure, add a section:

## Tenure arrangements at named places

One row per arrangement the source evidences:

- the place or point (spring, intake, weir, dam, canal reach, well field, river reach, lake,
  village) with its administrative location, its hydrological location and coordinates if given;
- the users, in plain words (officials by office; no private names);
- what they take or use, how much (numbers verbatim with units), and when (season, rotation group);
- who decides, allocates or operates; payment; labour; enforcement, as reported;
- how officials treat the use (permitted, unlicensed, tolerated, banned), attributed to whoever says
  so;
- conflicts, and who is excluded (women, tenants, tail-end users, landless people, fishers);
- candidate FAO tenure types (agency-control, modern-permit, regulatory-licence,
  water-supply-contract, commonhold, investment-contract, de-minimis, exempt-declared-commercial,
  reserve-minimum-flow, traditional-formal-right, customary, religious-law, informal, assumed,
  impossible, unrecognised): candidates only, because legality is not yours to decide;
- the pinpoint (page, table, line, article) for every row.

Also list the contradictions inside the source (figures that disagree) and its audited absences
(what the source should say and does not).
